WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:03.410
- [Narrator] Clinical
trials for COVID-19 vaccines

00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:05.350
are nearing the final stretch.

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:08.260
But health experts say
vaccines are just one piece

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:10.990
of a larger puzzle in
fighting the deadly disease

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:12.810
and getting us back to normal.

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:15.200
The other big piece are
effective treatments

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.040
for those who are a%lready sick.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.890
- Not everybody mounts
a good immune response,

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:23.280
in particular immunosuppress
patients are well-known

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.930
to not to respond well to vaccines.

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:27.550
So we are gonna need to have therapies

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:29.280
for those who are not vaccinated

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.820
and for those who don't
make a good immune response.

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:34.220
- [Narrator] Right now, there
are three main categories

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:37.690
of COVID-19 treatments:
antibody therapies,

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:41.020
immunomodulators, and antivirals.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:44.470
Antibody therapies leverage
antibodies, like these,

00:00:44.470 --> 00:00:46.210
that the body naturally produces

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:48.250
after recovering from a disease.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:50.910
To ward off infection,
they attach to proteins

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:52.400
on the viruses surface.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.250
That prevents it from infiltrating cells.

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:57.150
Different types of antibodies target

00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.140
different parts of the virus.

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:01.530
They circulate in the
yellowish liquid part of blood

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:02.800
known as plasma.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.620
That's where the treatment comes in.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:08.130
- You can take that liquid
and give it to a person

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:10.750
who's sick or to prevent disease.

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.140
So it is the transfer of immunity

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.940
from a person who's made it
to a person who's susceptible.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:18.530
- [Narrator] When given to sick patients,

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:20.780
convalescent plasma can potentially boost

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.030
a person's immune response to the virus,

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:24.900
but just for a few weeks.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.810
- We always pull it out during a pandemic.

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:30.780
We did this for 2009 H1N1,
obviously for things such as

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:33.620
Ebola virus disease in Africa.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:35.950
- [[Narrator] Because it's
relatively easy to produce

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:38.490
compared to vaccines and other drugs,

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:42.220
experts think it could possibly
be used both as a treatment

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:45.090
and as a preventative
medicine against COVID-19.

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:47.691
- It's based on the same principle

00:01:47.691 --> 00:01:49.730
of if you have a protective antibody,

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:53.220
passive transfer of that could
provide not only protection

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.680
prophylactically but also treatment.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.460
- [Narrator] Plasma is
widely thought to be safe,

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:01.150
and in August the FDA authorized using it

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:04.070
to treat patients
hospitalized with COVID-19.

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:06.870
But its effectiveness
is still being studied.

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.990
- Convalescent plasma
is a little bit of a,

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:09.920
it's a mixed bag.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:13.410
We don't actually know
the titers of the patients

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:16.010
who have survived and
done well after COVID.

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:18.770
- [Narrator] Titers are a
measurement of how many antibodies

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:20.440
are in a person's blood.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:23.040
Typically, the more
antibodies a person has,

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.660
the better chance they have
at fighting an infection.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:28.840
This means that not all
plasma is created equal.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:32.360
The amount and quality of
antibodies it contains can vary.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:35.211
That impacts how effective
the treatment can be.

00:02:35.211 --> 00:02:38.090
- Getting with convalescent
plasma right now

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:39.860
is a bit of an unknown.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:41.170
- [Narrator] To overcome this,

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:43.660
scientists use plasma as a blueprint

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:47.690
to build a more potent drug
known as monoclonal antibodies.

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:51.170
They do this by homing
in on specific antibodies

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:53.940
that do the best job
in stopping the virus.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.780
- We screen these huge
panels of antibodies

00:02:56.780 --> 00:03:00.920
through many in vitro assays
to select the best antibiotics

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:03.720
that block the ability of
the virus to get inside cells

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:04.580
and infect them.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:05.960
- [Narrator] Once they're identified,

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:07.700
scientists can mass produce them

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:10.030
in facilities that resemble breweries

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:13.050
and pack them into drugs
that act as a targeted attack

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:14.750
against the virus.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:16.897
- You're growing these
cells in bioreactors,

00:03:16.897 --> 00:03:19.140
which are huge steel tanks,

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:21.670
and then as the cells grow,
they are pumping out antibody.

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:24.310
You are concentrating
that, and in purified form,

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:26.290
this is what you are
administering to patients.

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:28.800
- [Narrator] Both Regeneron
and competitor Eli Lilly

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:31.460
have been racing to bring
monoclonal antibody drugs

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:33.440
to market for COVID-19.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:36.200
The companies are testing
drugs that contain one

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:37.670
or two types of antibodies,

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:40.130
and researchers say
they could have benefits

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:41.680
over plasma-based treatments.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:45.310
- Monoclonal antibody
preparations are consistent.

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:47.750
They are consistent
from patient to patient.

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:49.210
They can be standardized,

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:53.370
and they are hopefully
easier to use than plasma

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:55.170
because of its properties.

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:56.700
- [Narrator] Each of the
companies has released

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:59.810
encouraging data from
early stage clinical trials

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:02.660
showing their drugs help
reduce coronavirus levels

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:05.950
and improved symptoms in
non-hospitalized patients.

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:08.410
But both drug makers had
to pause their studies

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:10.200
in hospitalized patients,

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:11.660
and the Lilly study was halted

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:13.950
in this patient population altogether

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:15.780
because they didn't see a benefit.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:18.930
Early data among less severe
patients though looks promising

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:20.030
for both drugs.

00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:22.590
In October, President Trump was given

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:24.630
Regeneron's antibody cocktail

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:26.950
as part of his COVID-19 treatment.

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:29.610
The president said the drug
was key to his recovery,

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:32.570
but it's unclear how beneficial
the drug actually was.

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:34.980
- I just wanna say we have Regeneron.

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:38.730
We have a very similar
drug from Eli Lilly.

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:41.440
And they're coming out and
we're trying to get them

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:43.040
on an emergency basis.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:45.830
- [Narrator] Both Regeneron
and Lilly have asked the FDA

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:48.810
to authorize emergency
use of their treatments.

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:51.840
- We have committed to
manufacturing about 300,000 dose

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.150
of these antibodies by
the end of this year,

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:55.800
And we have also partnered with those

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:56.867
so that we can scale up and produce

00:04:56.867 --> 00:04:59.980
about 2 million dose of these antibodies

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:04.260
to be available annually
for worldwide use.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:06.160
- [Narrator] But physicians
have expressed concerns

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:07.320
about these new drugs.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:10.680
- This isn't the type of
drug that is always able

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:15.160
to be scaled, that can be made
available to everyone rapidly

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:18.130
and also is a low cost

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:19.590
- [Narrator] Experts say both plasma

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:22.040
and monoclonal antibody
treatments could help

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:23.780
not just COVID-19 patients

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:26.790
but also help prevent
infection in healthy people.

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:28.000
To treat patients,

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.950
doctors have also been
using immunomodulators.

00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:33.080
These drugs suppress the immune system

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:35.220
and ensuing inflammation in the sickest

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:37.010
of COVID-19 patients.

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:40.310
That's important because this
inflammation can be deadly.

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:43.550
One of those drugs is the
steroid Dexamethasone.

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:45.910
Researchers have found
that it improves survival

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:48.360
when given to severely ill patients.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:51.040
The other treatment being
used to fight COVID-19

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:53.580
is the antiviral drug Remdesivir.

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:55.870
This was another drug
the president was given

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:57.360
at Walter Reed Hospital.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:00.240
- So we really try to give
Remdesivir as soon as somebody

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:02.220
may be admitted to the hospital.

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:03.960
We wanna give it as early as possible.

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:06.240
We know from Tamiflu and the flu

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:07.930
and all those kinds of different drugs

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:10.240
that giving antivirals
sooner rather than later

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:11.570
is really important.

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:13.750
- [Narrator] Here's how
it stops the coronavirus.

00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:15.650
When a virus infects a cell,

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:19.030
it uses the cell's machinery
to build more viruses.

00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:21.660
It does that by making copies of its RNA.

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:22.990
That's the genetic material

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:25.163
that contains instructions
for replication.

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:29.330
Remdesivir is designed to
infiltrate these instructions

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:33.150
by inserting itself into copies
of the viruses genetic code.

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:35.410
This interrupts the replication process

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:37.860
and acts as a kill switch for the virus.

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:40.750
Promising clinical trials led
to the drug being approved

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:43.030
for emergency use in May,

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:45.800
but more recent studies have
produced conflicting results

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:47.960
about its ability to speed up recovery

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:50.630
and curve death in COVID-19 patients.

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:52.800
The Department of Health
and Human Services

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:55.230
allowed drug maker
Gilead to market the drug

00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:57.280
directly to hospitals in October.

00:06:57.280 --> 00:07:00.680
And the CEO said he was confident
in being able to deliver

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:02.280
the drug to the patients who need it.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:05.860
- We now have enough supply
of this medicine for patients

00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:09.280
in the United States and globally
by the end of this month.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:10.750
- [Narrator] There's
been tremendous progress

00:07:10.750 --> 00:07:13.810
in treating COVID-19 but
health experts caution

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:15.670
that there is still more work to be done

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:18.160
to see just how effective
these treatments are.

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:21.190
And to save lives, doctors
will need a multitude of drugs

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:22.023
at their disposal.

00:07:22.023 --> 00:07:24.740
- I think that we're gonna
see many more therapeutics

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:26.830
come out by the end of this pandemic,

00:07:26.830 --> 00:07:29.780
and so that people that enter
the hospital will at least

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:34.780
receive one specific COVID-19
treatment for their disease.

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:37.330
- [Narrator] Experts say these
three types of treatments

00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:40.380
are promising and they are
hopeful that therapeutic drugs

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:42.900
combined with a vaccine may finally put up

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:45.389
an effective defense against the pandemic.

00:07:45.389 --> 00:07:48.064
(upbeat instrumental music)

